<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Myles Coverdale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1488–<a href="/years/1569">1569</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Miles Coverdale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Myles Coverdale was an English reformer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> translator and preacher who in 1535 produced the first printed translation of the complete Bible into Early Modern English, completing the work of William Tyndale. A Cambridge-educated Augustinian friar, he left the order to preach reform and in 1528 fled to the Continent; knowing little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> or Greek, he translated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and German sources and from Tyndale. He oversaw the Great Bible of 1539, and his Psalms are still used in the 1662 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Exeter from 1551, he was ejected under Mary I in 1553 and went into exile again, contributing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Bible, before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1559.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/myles-coverdale.jpg" alt="Myles Coverdale" width="330" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myles_Coverdale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bale</a> gives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> as his birthplace. He became a bachelor of canon law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> in 1513 and was ordained priest in Norwich in 1514. Among the Augustinian friars of Cambridge he came under the influence of their prior, Robert Barnes, who had studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a>, and in 1526 he went with other friars to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> when Barnes was tried for heresy before Cardinal Thomas Wolsey. By Lent 1528 he was preaching in Essex against transubstantiation, the veneration of images and confession to a priest, views that were then very dangerous.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> he probably helped William Tyndale with his translations. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> of 1535 was most likely printed there, financed by Jacobus van Meteren, and dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>. In 1538 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cromwell">Thomas Cromwell</a> sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to oversee the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> of the Great Bible by François Regnault, but English bishops and theologians of the Sorbonne interfered, and Coverdale rescued sheets and type and brought them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where Richard Grafton and Edward Whitchurch finished the work.</p><p>When Cromwell, who had protected him, was executed in 1540, Coverdale left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with his wife, the Scotswoman Elizabeth Macheson. He lived in Strasbourg, took a doctorate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, and from 1543 was assistant minister and schoolmaster at Bergzabern, where he learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>. Returning in 1548, he became almoner to Catherine Parr and in 1549 went as chaplain with Lord John Russell to put down the Prayer Book Rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, staying on to preach there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 1559, he lodged with the Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>. His opposition to vestments was one reason he was not given back his bishopric, and from 1564 to 1566 he was rector of St Magnus the Martyr. He died in poverty in London on 20 January 1569. Besides his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalter</a>, used in musical settings such as Handel's Messiah, he compiled Ghostly Psalms and Spiritual Songs, which Celia Hughes calls the first English hymn book; it was banned in 1539, and one complete copy survives at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rutgers-university">Queen's College</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. The Episcopal Church (USA) honours him with Tyndale on 6 October.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Myles Coverdale” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Myles Coverdale</b> made the first translation in 1539; Frederick H. Hedge's of 1853 and Thomas Carlyle's became the most popular outside Lutheranism, while North American Lutherans long sang a composite translation from the 1868 Pennsylvania Lutheran Church Book and, more recently, that of the 1978 Lutheran Book of…</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-mighty-fortress-is-our-god">A Mighty Fortress Is Our God</a></li><li><blockquote>Jerome is associated with three Latin psalters, and in England the first Book of Common Prayer used <b>Myles Coverdale</b>'s prose rendering.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-23">Psalm 23</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.96,-1.08&amp;year=1540" aria-label="Yorkshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4526 312 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Myles Coverdale"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4976 582h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4976 582h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.96,-1.08&amp;year=1540">What was here in 1540? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
